WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.400
Hello. Today we're in the Polygon Samurai&nbsp;
Empire pack and I'm going to be showing you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.080
a couple of things about setting up and&nbsp;
using the Unity terrains. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.400
a couple of troubleshooting tips. If your terrain&nbsp;
is shiny like you see here, then what you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.000
to do is go to the base texture and change the&nbsp;
compression from high quality to normal quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.720
And if you've opened up Unity 6 and you've got&nbsp;
this shadowy bug when you try to paint textures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.600
one of the things you can try to do is go to&nbsp;
your project settings and change from DX12 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.200
DX11. As for setting up the terrain itself, what&nbsp;
you want to do is create a 3D object terrain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.640
go to the terrain settings and change the scale&nbsp;
a lot lower because it's quite big by default,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.120
especially the height. Then what I like to do is&nbsp;
to change the terrain height because by default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.880
it is at its lowest point. So what you want&nbsp;
to do is raise it a little bit and then set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.880
height. You can shift click to get a sample of&nbsp;
what height you want and then just cover the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.320
whole terrain. Then as you can see here, you can&nbsp;
raise the terrain up and also dig down. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.440
also now a good opportunity to test your maximum&nbsp;
height and depth and tweak as necessary. Next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.200
we need to add some terrain textures. So, we're&nbsp;
going to in your project window, create a terrain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.680
layer, create a grass, for example, and then just&nbsp;
select the texture and the normal map. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.880
we're going to come back to the terrain settings&nbsp;
and add a new terrain layer. Add any extra layers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.280
that you might need. And you can use the&nbsp;
brushes tab to change how the tool paints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.000
onto the terrain. Here I've grabbed a couple of&nbsp;
buildings from the demo scene. And as you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.720
painting on a few different layers of different&nbsp;
textures really helps set it into the scene. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.440
can very quickly improve the look by adding in&nbsp;
some ground prefabs like rocks and gravels and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.560
tiles to add that 3D element that really sells it&nbsp;
as part of the world. For trees, you can either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.280
manually place the prefabs or you can go to the&nbsp;
tree tab and add the prefabs and use the terrain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.160
tools to quickly paint them in at different scales&nbsp;
and rotations. For grass, you can either use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.800
same technique if you have prefabs. Uh, in this&nbsp;
case, I'm using a grass texture and using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.680
builtin Unity terrain grass system. It's best to&nbsp;
paint a patch of grass first and then modify it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.400
from there before continuing to dress the rest of&nbsp;
the scene. You can also add them as a detail mesh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.120
but it does not support LOD groups, so it can be a&nbsp;
little heavier. For our maps, we do manually place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.520
all of our prefabs just to make sure it is in the&nbsp;
exact spot that we want it to be. But if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.240
making a bigger scene, it makes sense to use some&nbsp;
tools to help speed up the process. Final step to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.400
improve the look is to add some camera post&nbsp;
effects. If you're using the universal render&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.200
pipeline, you need to add a global volume to your&nbsp;
scene. And from there, you can add overrides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:35.600
And I'd recommend a baseline of bloom, color&nbsp;
adjustments, vignette, and lift gamma gain. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.600
all for today. I hope you have found this video&nbsp;
helpful, and good luck with your world creation.

